   "part": "Toward war",
   "text": "About one in eight people in Croatia were Serbs, the majority in some areas, among them the region around Knin. In July 1990, a Serb assembly at Srb declared the sovereignty and autonomy of the Serbs in Croatia. On the seventeenth of August 1990, roads around Knin were blocked with logs. The barricades became a symbol of the unrest known as the Log Revolution. From the nineteenth of August, Croatian Serb leaders held a vote on Serb sovereignty and autonomy. The organisers reported overwhelming support. In December, they proclaimed a Serb Autonomous Region of Krajina. Croatia's new constitution, in force from the twenty-second of December 1990, described Croatia as the national state of the Croatian nation, and the state of members of other nations and minorities, naming the Serbs first among them. Its fourteenth article guaranteed equality. Croatia's government said Serbs kept full civic rights. Krajina Serb leaders argued that the constitution stripped Serbs of their former status as a constituent people of the republic."
